1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,986
Think about everyday life for a second. When you

2
00:00:02,986 --> 00:00:05,836
slice a pizza into equal pieces, measure a plank

3
00:00:05,836 --> 00:00:08,415
of wood down to the last half centimetre, or

4
00:00:08,415 --> 00:00:11,062
split a dinner bill three ways, you are using

5
00:00:11,062 --> 00:00:14,048
rational numbers all the time. So let's dig into

6
00:00:14,048 --> 00:00:16,491
one of their most surprising secrets.

7
00:00:17,483 --> 00:00:20,044
Come sit down. Today we are going on a

8
00:00:20,044 --> 00:00:23,509
tiny expedition, right here on our number line, to

9
00:00:23,509 --> 00:00:27,275
find something that feels almost like magic: the density

10
00:00:27,275 --> 00:00:29,534
property of rational numbers.

11
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:34,225
Look at zero and one. They are neighbours. Successive

12
00:00:34,225 --> 00:00:36,779
integers. If I ask you for a whole number

13
00:00:36,779 --> 00:00:40,093
strictly between them, you would search and search and

14
00:00:40,093 --> 00:00:42,854
find nothing. It is a canyon with no integer

15
00:00:42,854 --> 00:00:46,858
bridge across it. But rational numbers, fractions, are far

16
00:00:46,858 --> 00:00:50,447
more adventurous than whole numbers. Let's see what they

17
00:00:50,447 --> 00:00:51,138
can do.

18
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:56,613
Fractions themselves are ancient. Egyptian and Babylonian traders were

19
00:00:56,613 --> 00:00:59,464
splitting bread and land into pieces like one half

20
00:00:59,464 --> 00:01:02,857
and two thirds, thousands of years ago. Later, the

21
00:01:02,857 --> 00:01:06,658
Greeks turned fractions into ratios and proportions. But the

22
00:01:06,658 --> 00:01:10,391
deeper question, exactly how many rational numbers are hiding

23
00:01:10,391 --> 00:01:13,717
between two others, was only answered rigorously in the

24
00:01:13,717 --> 00:01:18,197
eighteen seventies, when the mathematician Richard Dedekind was building

25
00:01:18,197 --> 00:01:21,048
a precise theory of numbers. He proved what we

26
00:01:21,048 --> 00:01:23,966
are about to discover ourselves: the gap is never

27
00:01:23,966 --> 00:01:24,984
truly empty.

28
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:29,054
Here is our first trick. Take the two numbers

29
00:01:29,054 --> 00:01:31,826
at the edges of the canyon, zero and one,

30
00:01:31,826 --> 00:01:35,421
and average them. Add them, and divide by two.

31
00:01:35,421 --> 00:01:38,192
Zero plus one over two gives us one half.

32
00:01:38,192 --> 00:01:41,114
Watch our explorer zoom in twice, and plant a

33
00:01:41,114 --> 00:01:43,885
flag exactly in the middle. One half is a

34
00:01:43,885 --> 00:01:48,154
brand new rational number, sitting right inside the canyon.

35
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,741
But why stop there? We can repeat the very

36
00:01:51,741 --> 00:01:54,672
same trick. Average zero and one half, and we

37
00:01:54,672 --> 00:01:57,672
land on one quarter. Average one half and one,

38
00:01:57,672 --> 00:02:00,535
and we land on three quarters. Zooming in four

39
00:02:00,535 --> 00:02:03,602
times reveals two more numbers hiding inside the same

40
00:02:03,602 --> 00:02:06,602
tiny canyon, and neither of them existed for whole

41
00:02:06,602 --> 00:02:07,761
numbers at all.

42
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:12,262
Now zoom in even further, to a microscopic scale.

43
00:02:12,262 --> 00:02:15,326
Bisect each of those little gaps again, and one

44
00:02:15,326 --> 00:02:19,511
eighth, three eighths, five eighths, and seven eighths all

45
00:02:19,511 --> 00:02:23,247
appear, one after another. Every single time we take

46
00:02:23,247 --> 00:02:26,535
an average, a brand new rational number is born,

47
00:02:26,535 --> 00:02:29,524
and there is nothing stopping us from doing this

48
00:02:29,524 --> 00:02:31,841
again, and again, forever.

49
00:02:32,833 --> 00:02:36,330
Let's use this trick for real. Suppose someone asks:

50
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:39,267
find a rational number between one quarter and one

51
00:02:39,267 --> 00:02:43,044
half. We do exactly what we practised. Average them:

52
00:02:43,044 --> 00:02:46,121
one quarter plus one half, over two, gives three

53
00:02:46,121 --> 00:02:49,408
eighths. Check it on the line, three eighths sits

54
00:02:49,408 --> 00:02:53,324
neatly between one quarter and one half. Problem solved.

55
00:02:54,317 --> 00:02:57,450
Here is a trickier one. Find a rational number

56
00:02:57,450 --> 00:03:00,881
between zero point three and zero point three two.

57
00:03:00,881 --> 00:03:04,164
These two are already very close together, but the

58
00:03:04,164 --> 00:03:07,446
trick never fails us. Average them, and we get

59
00:03:07,446 --> 00:03:10,952
zero point three one. It slots in perfectly. No

60
00:03:10,952 --> 00:03:14,682
matter how close two rational numbers already are, their

61
00:03:14,682 --> 00:03:17,368
average always finds room in between.

62
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:21,726
That is the density property. For any two rational

63
00:03:21,726 --> 00:03:25,086
numbers a and b, their average lies strictly between

64
00:03:25,086 --> 00:03:28,738
them, and it is rational too. Repeat this forever,

65
00:03:28,738 --> 00:03:31,221
and the canyon is not empty at all. It

66
00:03:31,221 --> 00:03:35,092
becomes an endless, glowing highway of numbers, fifty one

67
00:03:35,092 --> 00:03:38,086
over one hundred, five hundred and two over one

68
00:03:38,086 --> 00:03:42,176
thousand, eight hundred and ninety nine over one thousand.

69
00:03:42,176 --> 00:03:46,120
Between any two rational numbers, there live infinitely many

70
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:46,778
more.

71
00:03:47,783 --> 00:03:50,939
Now it's your turn! Find one rational number that

72
00:03:50,939 --> 00:03:55,007
lies strictly between one-fourth and one-half. Use the halfway

73
00:03:55,007 --> 00:03:58,093
trick you just learned. Pause the video and work

74
00:03:58,093 --> 00:03:58,794
it out!

75
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,596
Add them up: one-fourth plus one-half, divided by two.

76
00:04:13,596 --> 00:04:18,407
That gives us three-eighths! And three-eighths sits perfectly between

77
00:04:18,407 --> 00:04:20,331
one-fourth and one-half.

78
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:24,978
Let's quickly recap what we discovered today. First, the

79
00:04:24,978 --> 00:04:27,880
average of any two rational numbers always gives us

80
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:31,187
a brand new rational number, sitting exactly in between

81
00:04:31,187 --> 00:04:34,899
them. Second, we can repeat this trick forever, zooming

82
00:04:34,899 --> 00:04:38,004
in again and again, from halves, to quarters, to

83
00:04:38,004 --> 00:04:41,311
eighths, and beyond. Third, it works just as well

84
00:04:41,311 --> 00:04:44,416
for decimals, even when two numbers are already very

85
00:04:44,416 --> 00:04:48,870
close together. And that, together, is exactly what mathematicians

86
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:52,380
call the density property of rational numbers. Thank you

87
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:54,674
so much for watching, and I will see you

88
00:04:54,674 --> 00:04:56,024
in the next lesson!
